{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (larger when recent 30d trend is negative).",
    "Trend/lag error: the model reacts too slowly to sell-offs (underestimates downside momentum early in the horizon). Add extra negative adjustment for days 1–15 when the recent slope is falling.",
    "Over-optimistic mean reversion: forecasts revert upward too quickly after declines; realized paths stay lower for longer. Reduce mean-reversion strength (or delay it) beyond ~day 5–20.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: mid-horizon (≈days 10–25) errors are largest (forecast > actual). Use a bigger downward correction in this window, smaller at the very short end.",
    "Overshoot around local rebounds: when history shows a small rebound after a drop, the model extrapolates the rebound too strongly; cap upward moves and keep forecasts closer to the recent low/rolling average until a sustained uptrend is evident."
  ]
}
